As graded rational $A^n$-modules, $$\label{eq:framed-module}
 \mathcal M_n\simeq\operatorname{Ind}_A^{A^n}M_\bullet^{\mathrm{reg}}
        =\bigl(\mathcal O(A^n)\otimes M_\bullet^{\mathrm{reg}}\bigr)^A.$$ If $g_i\in A$ are the frame coordinates, with diagonal change of frame $g_i\mapsto g_i h^{-1}$, then the adjoint holonomy and degree-two coordinates act as $$\label{eq:framed-action}
 s_{i,\mathrm{ad}}=g_i\mathbf b(\gamma_i)g_i^{-1},
       \qquad e_i=g_i\mathbf e g_i^{-1}.$$

For a rigid enhanced kernel category with monoidal automorphism $\Phi$, the images of kernels compactly generate its presentable trace and generate its horizontal trace under finite colimits and retracts. Moreover, $$\label{eq:horizontal-trace}
 \operatorname{Map}([J],[J'])
   \simeq \int^{T}\operatorname{Map}(\Phi T*J,J'*T).$$ The formula applies to the matrix category with levels $I,K$. Each corner trace embeds fully faithfully in the matrix trace. At a closed point of degree $d$, the cyclic trace of its $d$ geometric factors is identified with the one-factor trace twisted by $\operatorname{Fr}^{d*}$. This identification respects level transfers and full-cycle slides.

The equivalence of Theorem <a href="#thm:enhanced-iwahori" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:enhanced-iwahori">14</a> identifies the Iwahori horizontal trace with $\operatorname{Coh}^L(Y_N)$, carrying $[\mathbf1_I]$ to $P_N$ and $[Z(V)]$ to $P_N\otimes V$. Sheaf duality $\mathbb D=\operatorname{RHom}(-,\mathcal O)$ is a duality on this coherent model, and $\mathbb DP_N\simeq P_N$.*


*Let $z\in Z(L)$ be the element of Theorem <a href="#thm:enhanced-iwahori" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:enhanced-iwahori">14</a>. Full-cycle slide on the representation factor is $V(zs)$. Hyperspecial transfer identifies $[S(V)]$ with $P_N^S\otimes V$, where $P_N^S$ is a retract of $P_N$. If $s_{\rm sph}$ denotes spherical holonomy, then $$\label{eq:coordinate-shift}
 s_{\rm sph}=zs.$$ For neutral labels this comparison respects representation morphisms, homogeneous spherical-kernel morphisms, and matrix operations obtained from slides by composition, contraction, and tensoring by neutral spectators. It also respects invariant holonomy functions coming from arbitrary representations of $L$.

There is an affine saturated $H$-invariant open neighborhood $U_H\subset H$ of the identity class such that the conjugation map $$\label{local:eq-slice-map}
 L\mathbin{\times}^{H}(tU_H)\longrightarrow L,
 \qquad [g,tu]\longmapsto gtu g^{-1},$$ is strongly étale over its image. More precisely, it is the pullback of the induced étale map $U_H/\!/H\to L/\!/L$ along the adjoint quotient. The analogous statement holds with $t$ replaced by $z^{-1}t$. The neighborhood can be chosen so that the transverse conjugation operator and all nonresonant blocks of the vector equations are invertible. It contains the entire unipotent locus of $H$. The identity point of $U_H/\!/H$ is the only point above the selected semisimple class; the corresponding fiber in $U_H$ still contains every unipotent element.

Over $U_H$, the flag space defining $P_N$ is the disjoint union, over $b$, of the derived schemes $$\label{local:eq-negative-flag}
 \{(B,u,N):(B,u)\in U_b,\ N\in W_B,
                   \ (\operatorname{Ad}(u)-1)N=0\text{ in }W_B\}.$$ The base $U_b$ is smooth of dimension $\dim H$. All derived structure in <a href="#local:eq-negative-flag" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="local:eq-negative-flag">[local:eq-negative-flag]</a> is in its displayed vector equation.

After shrinking the saturated slice $U_H$ around the identity class, there are an isomorphism $$\label{eq:spherical-line}
 L_S\simeq\mathcal O_Y$$ and an $H$-equivariant isomorphism of graded algebras over $\mathcal O(U_H)$ $$\label{eq:spherical-end}
 \operatorname{sh}H^\bullet(B_{\rm sph}^0)
   \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }
 \underline{\operatorname{End}}^{\bullet}_{Y}(L_S)
   =\mathcal O(Y).$$ The isomorphism is induced by spherical transfer, duality, and $G_0$. It respects the representation-valued operations of Proposition <a href="#prop:trace-model" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:trace-model">26</a>. As an isomorphism between the two equation models over $U_H$, it preserves the open orbit and its boundary over every unipotent $u\in U_H$.

There are morphisms $$\label{boundary:evaluation}
 \lambda_i:[\operatorname{Spec}R/A]\longrightarrow[Y_i/H_i^\natural]$$ whose field-valued images have unipotent residual holonomy and a vector in $E_i$ outside its open $H_i$-orbit. For every $n\geq1$, the product morphism $\lambda=(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n)$ is affine. Write $R_n$ for the $H^\natural$-equivariant $B$-algebra representing $\lambda_*\mathcal O$ on $Y$. There is an equivariant $B$-module map $$\label{eq:cyclic-module-map}
 R_n\longrightarrow H^0(F_n^{\mathrm{qc}})$$ that sends $1$ to the class of $f$ in the spherical summand. In particular, the composite $$\label{boundary:section-f}
 B\longrightarrow R_n\longrightarrow H^0(F_n^{\mathrm{qc}})
       \longrightarrow F_n^{\mathrm{qc}}$$ is the section defined by $f$.
